{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Since the onset of Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID19), SARS-CoV-2 infection has been circulating globally... The fact that SARS-CoV-2 is still mutating indicates that it remains a global health threat. The WHO study published in Nature [1, 3] also suggested that accurately tracking SARS-CoV-2 and its impact had been challenging.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the global circulation of SARS-CoV-2, the ongoing threat of mutation, and the challenge of accurately tracking the virus and its impact, establishing the current state of knowledge and uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "In addition, the advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates, so this study defined reinfection and included only literature with reinfection intervals greater than 90 days. Secondly, considering the heterogeneity of reinfection rates of different variants, we constructed a meta-regression of time-varying reinfection rates and compared it with the temporal spline regression without considering heterogeneity to explore the temporal trends of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rates, which could provide a basis for subsequent research on prediction of SARS-CoV-2 infection, and could guide significance for the future policy formulation of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection.",
      "reason": "The authors explain the need for the review by highlighting the limitations of previous studies (heterogeneity in definitions) and the specific methodological improvements (meta-regression, spline regression) they offer to better understand reinfection trends and guide policy."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "In addition, the advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates, so this study defined reinfection and included only literature with reinfection intervals greater than 90 days.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly acknowledge the existence of previous meta-analyses and explain the need for the current review by contrasting their methodology (specifically regarding reinfection definitions) with those previous studies."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a review of reinfection rates (observational data), not an intervention study, so a mechanism of action is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is not a review of a complex intervention or delivery context, so a logic model is not required."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, so as to provide reference for the development of global unified reinfection standards and reinfection measures in the future.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly state the objectives of the study, which are to describe/summarize current reinfection rates and to examine trends in reinfection rates over time."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a systematic review of observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional) estimating reinfection rates, not a review of intervention effects (e.g., randomized controlled trials comparing interventions), so PICO is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria were: (1) Conformed to research questions, i.e., studies that estimated the reinfection rate of SARS-CoV-2 or we could calculate it based on the data provided in the paper; (2) The study design included cohort study, case-control study, and descriptive study; (3) The study fited our working definition of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection... The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; (2) Belonged to one of the following types of studies: editorial, case report, case series study, systematic review, meta-analysis, animal experiment, news report; (3) Secondary reporting or articles on repeated studies of the same population; (4) Grey literature",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly lists inclusion criteria (research question, study design, working definition) and exclusion criteria (sample size, study type, secondary reporting, grey literature)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language. We conducted preliminary screening based on the English title and English abstract provided by nonEnglish articles. Further, in the screening process, if the included article was neither in English nor in Chinese and did not provide an English abstract or full text, we used an online translator (Google Translate) for translation.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies the criteria regarding language (no limitation, with translation procedures described)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not appear to have excluded studies specifically because outcomes were not measured versus not reported in a way that requires this distinction to be explicitly stated as a criterion; the exclusion criteria provided are based on study type, sample size, and publication type."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Subgroup analyses were performed by meta-regression to fit stratified rates of reinfection based on variables such as variant and country, and we also used spline regression to fit time-varying reinfection rates that did not account for heterogeneity.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies how studies were grouped for synthesis (subgroup analyses by age, variant, country, study population, and study type)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20... (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The manuscript provides rationales for restrictions, such as excluding sample sizes less than 20 and excluding grey literature (noted in the limitations section as well)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

